      en: "The Cartagena de Indias Convention Center, officially named after Julio César Turbay Ayala, is a premier landmark and the most important venue for international events in Colombia. Located on the beautiful Las Ánimas Bay, just outside the city's walled historic center, it was inaugurated in 1982 to provide a world-class space for summits, congresses, and cultural performances. Its unique architecture, with its prominent white-tiled roofs, has become a familiar part of the Cartagena skyline. The center has hosted some of the most significant political events in the region, including various Summits of the Americas and Nobel Prize ceremonies. Its versatile spaces include a grand auditorium for 1,500 people and numerous smaller halls and plazas. The center's location provides stunning views of the city's fortifications and the modern skyline of Bocagrande. It remains a key engine for the city's tourism industry and a symbol of Cartagena's status as a leading global destination for business and culture."
