      en: "The National Capitol of Colombia is a majestic neoclassical landmark that serves as the heart of the country's legislative power. Located on the southern side of the Plaza de Bolívar in Bogotá, its imposing facade of yellow sandstone and its grand ionic colonnade are a masterpiece of 19th-century architecture. The building's construction was a monumental task that spanned almost 80 years, from 1848 to 1926, reflecting the nation's political evolution and its enduring republican ideals. Inside, the Capitolio houses the chambers of the Senate and the House of Representatives, as well as the Elliptical Hall, where the President of Colombia is traditionally inaugurated. The building is decorated with numerous statues, murals, and historic artifacts that tell the story of the nation's struggle for independence and its democratic journey. Its lack of a dome is a distinctive architectural feature that adds to its unique character. It stands as a symbol of the strength and continuity of the Colombian state and its democratic institutions."
